index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..6eb4ae5e2aed882cad4415f326ffc7242480742a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/AGENT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,252 @@
+# FreeBSD port: build, verify, package, install
+
+This file documents the community FreeBSD/amd64 port and its optional per-IP NetFlow collector. It is intended for maintainers and coding assistants. It contains no deployment-specific configuration. This is not an officially supported New Relic distribution.
+
+## Invariants
+
+- Treat `go.mod` as authoritative. The current source requires Go 1.27.2. Dependencies are module-based; there is no checked-in `vendor` directory. Use `-mod=readonly`; do not change dependency pins or run `go mod vendor` as part of a build.
+- Build the agent with `CGO_ENABLED=0`. The optional exporter is a separate C program; it does not make the agent depend on CGO.
+- Keep toolchains, caches, external sources and release outputs in a separate build directory. Do not modify an appliance's package repositories or replace its base libraries.
+- Never commit license keys, live configuration, account/entity IDs, captured production traffic or private topology. Package only the credential-free `.sample` file.
+- Use native FreeBSD APIs and genuine counters. Unsupported metrics must be absent, not fabricated as zero. Retain Linux/Darwin/Windows behavior when changing shared code.
+- Source publication and release uploads are separate, explicit actions. Building never pushes anything.
+
+## What the port changed
+
+Platform implementations live in the usual OS-suffixed files: configuration and effective-UID handling; startup/PID-file locking; hostname, distro, fingerprint and hardware inventory; Unix signal/IPC/shutdown handling; integration process execution; CPU/load/memory/swap, storage, network and process collection. FreeBSD registers its own plugins in `pkg/plugins/plugins_freebsd.go`.
+
+`pkg/metrics/storage/devstat_freebsd.go` uses `SysctlRaw` and validates the amd64 devstat-v6 layout. A binary sysctl must not go through a string API that removes trailing NUL bytes. Mounted-filesystem capacity and physical-device I/O are different measurements: GEOM labels/partitions must not duplicate whole-device I/O. PID reuse and counter resets must not produce negative or inflated rates.
+
+`pkg/metrics/system_sample_freebsd.go` omits unavailable attributes. Private availability flags survive the generic metric truncation path: `pkg/helpers/metric/metric.go` copies the complete struct only when needed, then transforms exported fields. Keep the regression test that serializes a truncated real SystemSample; JSON-only tests do not exercise this pipeline boundary.
+
+The optional flow feature consists of:
+
+- `internal/netflow`: bounded NetFlow v9 template/record decoder, IPv4/IPv6, exporter/source-ID isolation, duplicate/restart/wrap handling and actual softflowd wire fixtures.
+- `pkg/metrics/netflow`: context-owned UDP listener, per-IP endpoint deltas, CIDR selection, bounded aggregation and collector diagnostics.
+- `pkg/config/netflow.go` and the FreeBSD plugin registration: disabled-by-default configuration and startup error propagation.
+- `build/package/freebsd/newrelic-netflow-exporter.sh`: optional native exporter service. Observe ONE internal pre-NAT interface, never loopback.
+
+Do not replace this with polling `pfctl -ss`: states can appear and disappear between polls. Native pflow exports state records, but its 30-second batch flush is not a periodic active-state accounting interval. The bundled exporter explicitly expires flow segments at a bounded lifetime.
+
+## Isolated native build environment
+
+Run the following on a FreeBSD/amd64 build host, from the repository root. Ordinary FreeBSD installations may already have `cc`; stripped appliances may not.
+
+```sh
+export BUILD_ROOT=/var/tmp/newrelic-freebsd-build
+mkdir -p "$BUILD_ROOT"
+fetch -o "$BUILD_ROOT/go.tar.gz" https://go.dev/dl/go1.27.2.freebsd-amd64.tar.gz
+test "$(sha256 -q "$BUILD_ROOT/go.tar.gz")" = 3baf61eec549b0c3f638d82636388e36abf94aa14814a011f011d2648ad1eded
+tar -xzf "$BUILD_ROOT/go.tar.gz" -C "$BUILD_ROOT"
+export GO_BIN="$BUILD_ROOT/go/bin/go"
+export GOCACHE="$BUILD_ROOT/go-cache"
+export GOMODCACHE="$BUILD_ROOT/go-modules"
+export GOPATH="$BUILD_ROOT/go-path"
+export GOMAXPROCS=2
+export GOFLAGS=-p=2
+export GOTOOLCHAIN=local
+export CGO_ENABLED=0
+"$GO_BIN" version
+```
+
+The checksums here pin the documented toolchain. When upgrading Go, update the requirement and checksum from the official Go download metadata together.
+
+For a source checkout, record build metadata before copying it to a host without Git:
+
+```sh
+export GIT_COMMIT="$(git rev-parse HEAD)"
+export SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH="$(git show -s --format=%ct HEAD)"
+```
+
+Pass these environment values to the native build host along with the source tree if its copy omits `.git`. They identify the base commit and build metadata; uncommitted changes still need to be retained with the release source. Do not invent a clean upstream commit for modified code.
+
+## Build and test the agent
+
+```sh
+"$GO_BIN" build -mod=readonly -buildvcs=false -trimpath \
+  -ldflags "-X main.buildVersion=1.80.6-freebsd.2 -X main.gitCommit=$GIT_COMMIT" \
+  -o "$BUILD_ROOT/bin/" ./cmd/...
+
+"$GO_BIN" test -mod=readonly -timeout 10m \
+  ./cmd/newrelic-infra/initialize ./internal/plugins/freebsd \
+  ./pkg/helpers/metric ./pkg/metrics ./pkg/metrics/storage \
+  ./pkg/metrics/process ./pkg/metrics/network ./pkg/config \
+  ./pkg/sysinfo/hostname ./internal/netflow ./pkg/metrics/netflow ./pkg/plugins
+```
+
+A non-FreeBSD host with Go can cross-compile the three Go binaries using `GOOS=freebsd GOARCH=amd64 CGO_ENABLED=0`; it cannot execute the resulting binaries or prove kernel behavior. Native `pkg` creation must run on FreeBSD/amd64. A Linux cross-build of `./cmd/...` is a useful shared-code check, not a substitute for native smoke tests.
+
+## Build the optional exporter
+
+The stock softflowd 1.0.0 capture path can leave low-volume BPF traffic buffered indefinitely: libpcap receives packets while softflowd processes none. Do not make a test pass by sending filler packets. The pinned softflowd 1.1.1 source is built with `softflowd-freebsd.patch`, which:
+
+1. enables `pcap_set_immediate_mode` before activation on FreeBSD;
+2. includes templates in every NetFlow v9 datagram, so a collector restart or template-cache expiry does not discard the next quiet flow.
+
+The latter intentionally spends some export bandwidth to make loopback UDP exports self-describing. Both changes are required for the bundled exporter. Preserve its upstream license and the patch in redistributed artifacts.
+
+Install build tools using the build host's existing repositories:
+
+```sh
+pkg install -y gmake autoconf automake
+fetch -o "$BUILD_ROOT/softflowd-source.tar.gz" \
+  https://codeload.github.com/irino/softflowd/tar.gz/refs/tags/softflowd-v1.1.1
+```
+
+`build-exporter.sh` checks source SHA-256 `a6882e59931e5880901f8ee28d78b082cb3000ad8d28af35c13f2b528edbb2c9` before extraction and applies the patch. With a native compiler:
+
+```sh
+SOURCE_ARCHIVE="$BUILD_ROOT/softflowd-source.tar.gz" \
+OUTPUT_DIR="$BUILD_ROOT/exporter" \
+CC=cc build/package/freebsd/build-exporter.sh
+```
+
+If `cc` is absent, unpack an isolated Zig toolchain rather than changing the base system:
+
+```sh
+fetch -o "$BUILD_ROOT/zig.tar.xz" \
+  https://ziglang.org/download/0.17.0/zig-x86_64-freebsd-0.17.0.tar.xz
+test "$(sha256 -q "$BUILD_ROOT/zig.tar.xz")" = a314f1cc92d098bd39caf9e5607a0de2a8fd0d00064776cb3fd5b3bee38a67c9
+mkdir -p "$BUILD_ROOT/zig"
+tar -xf "$BUILD_ROOT/zig.tar.xz" -C "$BUILD_ROOT/zig" --strip-components 1
+SOURCE_ARCHIVE="$BUILD_ROOT/softflowd-source.tar.gz" \
+OUTPUT_DIR="$BUILD_ROOT/exporter" \
+CPU_FLAGS=-mcpu=x86_64 \
+CC="$BUILD_ROOT/zig/zig cc" build/package/freebsd/build-exporter.sh
+```
+
+Use a new empty output directory for each build; the script refuses to erase an existing output. The exporter dynamically uses the native base libpcap/libc. Do not claim the C binaries are statically linked or ABI-independent.
+
+The distribution CPU baseline is generic amd64: the script defaults `CPU_FLAGS` to `-march=x86-64` for native `cc`/Clang. The Zig recipe explicitly sets the equivalent `CPU_FLAGS=-mcpu=x86_64`, because Zig 0.17.0 rejects the canonical Clang spelling and `-mtune=generic`. Its compiler driver reports `-target-cpu x86-64` for this setting. These baseline settings do not require AVX and accommodate CPUs such as the Intel Celeron N4000; do not override them with build-host/native CPU tuning for distribution. CPU instruction compatibility is separate from the FreeBSD ABI and shared-library compatibility described above. See the [Clang architecture option](https://clang.llvm.org/docs/CommandGuide/clang.html#cmdoption-march) and [x86 baseline definition](https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/x86-Options.html).
+
+Before publishing, smoke-test both `softflowd` and `softflowctl` natively on the intended FreeBSD target, including a baseline CPU without AVX: exercise exporter startup, control-socket queries and a real flow arriving at the collector. A successful build on a newer CPU or a matching `FreeBSD:16:amd64` package ABI alone does not establish CPU compatibility. Previously built exporter payloads may inherit stronger build-host CPU requirements; rebuild and verify them rather than assuming this source change makes existing binaries portable.
+
+## Create a shareable release
+
+On the native build host, after the exporter build:
+
+```sh
+GO_BIN="$GO_BIN" \
+EXPORTER_DIR="$BUILD_ROOT/exporter" \
+OUTPUT_DIR="$BUILD_ROOT/release-1.80.6-freebsd.2" \
+VERSION=1.80.6-freebsd.2 PKG_VERSION=1.80.6_2 \
+GIT_COMMIT="$GIT_COMMIT" SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH="$SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH" \
+build/package/freebsd/release.sh pkg
+```
+
+This creates raw Go binaries under `bin/`, an install-tree tar.gz, a native `.pkg`, and `SHA256SUMS`. `release.sh tar` creates the archive without requiring `pkg`; the exporter inputs must already be native FreeBSD binaries. Archives include the patched exporter/control utility under `usr/local/libexec/newrelic-infra`, project/third-party licenses, the exporter patch/build provenance, sample config, service scripts and this file. No live config, state, account-specific data or keys belong in these artifacts.
+
+Checksums identify the delivered files. They are not signatures, and the tar build is not advertised as byte-for-byte reproducible. Verify after copying artifacts:
+
+```sh
+shasum -a 256 -c SHA256SUMS
+```
+
+## Install and configure
+
+As root on the matching FreeBSD ABI:
+
+```sh
+pkg add ./newrelic-infra-freebsd-1.80.6_2.pkg
+```
+
+The package creates a root:wheel 0600 live configuration from the empty-license sample only when no configuration exists. Existing keys/configuration and runtime state must survive installation and upgrade. It stops supervised processes before replacing binaries, avoiding `ETXTBSY`. A fresh package must not start a credential-free sample. Uninstall retains modified configuration and collected state.
+
+The corrected local payload is in `dist/freebsd-1.80.6_2/`. Package revision `_1` included exporter binaries with build-host AVX instructions and failed with `Illegal instruction` on a Celeron N4000; use `_2`, not the older package or its install-tree archive. The Go agent release remains `1.80.6-freebsd.2`. Run pfSense command examples as root in `sh`, and verify the target ABI before installing; do not force an ABI mismatch or change appliance repositories.
+
+Set the account's ingest license key in `/usr/local/etc/newrelic-infra.yml`; a user API key is not an ingest key.
+
+`NEW_RELIC_API_KEY`, `NEW_RELIC_ACCOUNT_ID` and `NEW_RELIC_REGION` from New Relic's generic CLI installer are installer inputs, not Infrastructure Agent YAML settings. A user API key (`NRAK-...`) can query an authorized account's ingest license key through NerdGraph; it must not be substituted for `license_key` or persisted on the monitored host. Copy the ingest key from the intended account. The agent derives its EU backend from that key; changing the key redirects future telemetry, not historical data. Do not run the generic Linux installer over this native FreeBSD installation.
+
+```sh
+sysrc -f /etc/rc.conf.local newrelic_infra_enable=YES
+service newrelic-infra.sh start
+service newrelic-infra.sh status
+curl http://localhost:8003/v1/status/errors
+```
+
+Enable `status_server_enabled: true` if using those local health endpoints. Bindings to `localhost` may resolve to IPv6; a failed request to 127.0.0.1 alone is not proof that the localhost service is down.
+
+For optional traffic telemetry, merge a `netflow` block into the live YAML:
+
+```yaml
+netflow:
+  enabled: true
+  listen: "127.0.0.1:2055"
+  sample_interval: 15
+  max_ips: 8192
+  observation_point: "pre-nat-lan"
+  tracked_cidrs: []
+```
+
+An empty CIDR list reports every observed endpoint; restrict it to internal prefixes to control cardinality/privacy. `max_ips` is per reporting window, not a global distinct-IP limit. Restart the agent, select the correct internal interface, and start the bundled exporter:
+
+```sh
+service newrelic-infra.sh restart
+LAN_INTERFACE=igb1  # example only: select the actual internal observation boundary
+sysrc -f /etc/rc.conf.local newrelic_netflow_enable=YES \
+  newrelic_netflow_interface="$LAN_INTERFACE"
+service newrelic-netflow-exporter.sh start
+service newrelic-netflow-exporter.sh status
+/usr/local/libexec/newrelic-infra/softflowctl \
+  -c /var/run/newrelic-infra/softflowd.ctl statistics
+```
+
+The `.sh` service suffix is intentional: pfSense discovers these scripts at boot. The exporter waits for the actual agent PID to own the local UDP listener; `sockstat` truncates command names, so matching its displayed program name is insufficient. `daemon` supervises the agent; softflowd daemonizes itself. Neither service changes firewall rules, gateways, NAT or routing.
+
+Enabling `netflow.enabled` starts only the collector. The exporter has its own enable flag and must be running before traffic events can exist. `tracked_cidrs` filters observed endpoints; listing several LAN/VLAN prefixes does not capture their separate interfaces. This service observes one interface. Do not sum overlapping observations or claim coverage of other interfaces.
+
+### Diagnose missing telemetry
+
+1. Confirm both services and the actual agent child process, not just `service newrelic-infra.sh status`: that status can refer to `daemon` while the agent exits and restarts every five seconds.
+2. If neither the status server nor UDP collector starts, inspect the startup log. On pfSense this is `/var/log/system.log`; license validation can fail before `/var/log/newrelic-infra/newrelic-infra.log` is initialized. `invalid license` is a local format rejection, not a backend authentication response. Do not remove punctuation from an unrelated API key to bypass validation; obtain the correct ingest key.
+3. Check `curl --max-time 10 http://localhost:8003/v1/status/errors` and `sockstat -4 -l -P udp -p 2055`. The status endpoints require `status_server_enabled: true`; test `localhost`, which can resolve to IPv6.
+4. Query `softflowctl ... statistics` as above. Processed packets should increase with observed IP traffic; exports appear after flow expiry. `Illegal instruction` requires a corrected CPU-compatible exporter build, not a firewall-rule change. Inspect libpcap/interface drops as well as export failures.
+5. In the intended New Relic account, inspect `PfFlowCollectorSample`: events with zero `datagramsReceived` mean the reporting collector has received no exporter traffic. Positive `recordsDecoded` without traffic rows calls for checking `tracked_cidrs` and query filters; include backend ingest delay before concluding the rows were lost.
+6. Discover actual `hostname` and `observationPoint` values before copying a filtered dashboard query. The sample configuration uses `router-lan`, whereas this guide's opt-in example uses `pre-nat-lan`; neither is automatically renamed. Match the live YAML and the reporting hostname, not the SSH alias or UI display name.
+
+```sql
+FROM PfFlowCollectorSample
+SELECT sum(datagramsReceived), sum(recordsDecoded),
+       sum(malformedDatagrams), sum(unknownTemplates),
+       sum(sequenceGaps), sum(droppedIPs)
+FACET hostname, observationPoint
+SINCE 15 minutes ago
+```
+
+For the download leaderboard, use `sum(bytesReceived)`, not the sum of both directions. Start with MiB so a small, newly observed transfer is readable; use a table or bar chart:
+
+```sql
+FROM PfIpTrafficSample
+SELECT sum(bytesReceived) / 1048576 AS 'Received MiB'
+WHERE hostname = 'router.example.invalid'
+  AND observationPoint = 'pre-nat-lan'
+FACET ip
+SINCE 15 minutes ago
+LIMIT 20
+```
+
+Replace the example hostname and observation point with discovered values. This includes router-local and inter-subnet traffic crossing the capture boundary, not exclusively Internet downloads. A newly enabled exporter has no retrospective traffic history.
+
+## Telemetry semantics and smoke requirements
+
+- `PfIpTrafficSample` contains non-overlapping deltas: `bytesSent`, `bytesReceived`, `packetsSent`, `packetsReceived`, `flowRecords`, `intervalMs`, `ip`, `observationPoint`.
+- Directions are endpoint-relative. The source sends; the destination receives. `flowRecords` is exported records, not TCP connections. IPv4 and IPv6 addresses are canonicalized.
+- This is IP-layer interface observation, not Ethernet utilization or proof a packet passed a firewall rule. It includes traffic to/from the router on the chosen boundary. It cannot see traffic that bypasses that interface or recover internal identities after NAT.
+- Capture one boundary. Feeding both WAN and LAN copies of the same packet into a collector double-counts traffic. A transfer between two reported endpoints appears at both endpoints; their combined sent+received volume is not a network-total counter.
+- Export latency includes flow expiry (30 seconds), exporter expiry checks (5 seconds), agent reporting (15 seconds), and backend ingest. Charts show accounted bytes in report-time buckets, not an interpolated packet-time trace. This is monitoring, not lossless billing.
+- Inspect `PfFlowCollectorSample` alongside traffic: malformed datagrams, unknown templates, duplicate datagrams, missing sequence units and rejected endpoint updates (`droppedIPs`). Sequence units can differ across exporter implementations. Diagnostics are not a guarantee against all BPF/UDP loss; inspect exporter/libpcap drop counters too.
+- A real routed smoke should use an isolated lab. For 1,000 IPv4 UDP request/reply pairs with 1,000/400-byte payloads, expected application-packet L3 deltas are 1,028,000 sent / 428,000 received and 1,000 packets each. With IPv6, 100 such pairs contribute 104,800 / 44,800 bytes; neighbor discovery or other control traffic can legitimately increase the interface totals. Do not relabel benchmark addresses as production hosts or change production default routes to fabricate visibility.
+- Verify exact totals in New Relic, a bandwidth time-series query, collector restart with the exporter still running, package install/upgrade preservation, and reboot autostart. Tests alone are not sufficient.
+
+Example graph query:
+
+```sql
+FROM PfIpTrafficSample
+SELECT rate(sum(bytesSent), 1 second) * 8 / 1000000 AS 'Sent Mbps',
+       rate(sum(bytesReceived), 1 second) * 8 / 1000000 AS 'Received Mbps'
+WHERE observationPoint = 'pre-nat-lan' AND ip = '192.0.2.10'
+TIMESERIES 30 seconds SINCE 30 minutes ago
+```
+
+When resuming an old test session, choose a query time window that actually contains the traffic. An empty recent query is not evidence of loss if the verified flow happened hours earlier.
